# Velox — SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for every version number in the project.
#
# Edit versions here, then:
#   ./Scripts/build.sh        # regenerates Sources/VeloxCore/Support/Versions.swift
#   ./Scripts/build-kernel.sh # rebuilds the kernel (if KERNEL_ORG_* / the fragment changed)
#   ./Scripts/make-guest.sh   # rebuilds the erofs guest rootfs (static dockerd + vinit)
#
# Nothing else in the repo should hard-code a version. See CLAUDE.md.
# Shell-sourceable (KEY=VALUE, no spaces) so every script and the Swift codegen
# read the exact same values.

# --- Velox itself ---
VELOX_VERSION=1.4.1
# GitHub repo the updater checks for new releases (owner/name).
VELOX_GITHUB_REPO=mikaelhug/Velox
# Privileged-port-helper revision. Bumped ONLY when velox-porthelper's binary or socket protocol
# changes and an already-installed helper must be replaced. Deliberately independent of
# VELOX_VERSION so a normal app update doesn't re-trigger the one-time admin (osascript) install.
# 2: helper gained the route add/del command + the install also writes /etc/resolver (named access).
# 3: route targets restricted to RFC-1918 private space (hardening; no protocol change).
# 4: helper gained the ipfwd command — restore net.inet.ip.forwarding=1 (restore-only; some VPN
#    clients zero it on connect, which kills vmnet NAT egress for every container).
# 5: helper gained the tcp6/udp6 bind verbs — an [::1] loopback listener for privileged (<1024)
#    ports, so a published :80/:443 is reachable via `localhost` (which resolves to ::1 first),
#    not only 127.0.0.1.
# 6: bind verbs gained the optional `any` argument — bind 0.0.0.0/[::] instead of loopback,
#    so a published privileged port (-p 22:22, -p 80:80) is reachable from other machines,
#    matching Docker's default. Opt-in per request; a bare "tcp <port>" still binds loopback.
PORTHELPER_REVISION=8   # 8: SO_REUSEADDR restored on wildcard binds (privileged -p 80:80 could
                        #    not rebind for ~60s after any client connection, and the client's
                        #    loopback fallback then silently made it host-only)
                        # 7: `resolver <port>` verb — the helper writes /etc/resolver at runtime
# Ed25519 public key (base64 raw, 32 bytes) that release .zips are signed with. The private
# key lives ONLY in the GitHub Actions secret VELOX_ED25519_PRIVATE_KEY; CI signs each zip
# (Scripts/release-sign.swift) and the updater refuses unsigned/invalid archives when this
# key is set. Empty ⇒ dev build, verification skipped. One-time key generation:
#   swift Scripts/release-sign.swift keygen
# then paste the printed PUBLIC key here and store the PRIVATE key as the repo secret.
VELOX_RELEASE_PUBKEY=uu477O7tlHtYMJfbmBMFJmBFwgE+s+6VsSWbgUPhFK4=

# --- Guest OS: the Linux kernel (the VM's "OS version") ---
# Velox builds its OWN bare, fast arm64 kernel straight from kernel.org source
# (no LinuxKit), under full config control — an OrbStack-style "macvirt" kernel.
# Scripts/build-kernel.sh fetches this exact tarball, verifies the SHA-256 below,
# and compiles an uncompressed arch/arm64/boot/Image that VZLinuxBootLoader boots
# directly. Bump = edit these two lines (get the sum from the signed
# https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v<MAJOR>.x/sha256sums.asc — e.g. v7.x for 7.x).
# The kernel.org major dir (v<MAJOR>.x) is derived from the version by build-kernel.sh,
# so there is no separate series var.
KERNEL_ORG_VERSION=7.2.2
KERNEL_ORG_SHA256=7d0e7ce14f98c43efe880cffbf354a59be45928fdf7170d7333c374ae91c0d83

# --- Guest userspace: a flat, fully-static appliance (NO LinuxKit, NO dind) ---
# The guest is a single erofs root image built by Scripts/make-guest.sh from
# guest/rootfs/Dockerfile: the static Docker server binaries (this version),
# a static mkfs.ext4, the CA bundle, and the static Rust /sbin/init (guest/vinit).
# DOCKER_VERSION pins BOTH the in-VM daemon AND the Mac client (same version, two
# channels), so `docker` on the host and `dockerd` in the guest never skew:
#   guest daemon:  https://download.docker.com/linux/static/stable/aarch64/docker-<ver>.tgz  (guest/rootfs/Dockerfile)
#   mac client:    https://download.docker.com/mac/static/stable/<arch>/docker-<ver>.tgz     (Scripts/build-app.sh)
# MAJOR-BUMP CHECKLIST: vinit's direct (named) access inserts gateway-allow rules into
# dockerd-29's two host→container drop chains BY NAME (`docker-bridges` table,
# `filter-FORWARD` + `raw-PREROUTING` — guest/vinit/src/main.rs, assert_direct_access_rules).
# A new dockerd major can rename/restructure these — re-verify them (and the nftables
# firewall backend flags) before bumping past 29.x, or named access silently breaks.
# ANY bump (major or minor) must also update the two SHA-256 pins below — Docker publishes
# no checksum sidecar for the static tarballs, so we pin them here (like KERNEL_ORG_SHA256):
#   curl -fsSL <url> | shasum -a 256
DOCKER_VERSION=29.7.2
# linux/static/stable/aarch64/docker-<ver>.tgz — the guest dockerd (guest/rootfs/Dockerfile)
DOCKER_STATIC_SHA256=43d143448adf2c2787704e7d7704fd6d62d367a54c5edaef0a3f75509cb0938d
# mac/static/stable/aarch64/docker-<ver>.tgz — the bundled mac client (Scripts/build-app.sh)
DOCKER_CLI_MAC_ARM64_SHA256=b8683ed19d1f06048a496f9b8429e2c71d0b088d475b7487c054ea3666c02a3c

# --- Guest multi-arch emulation: QEMU user-mode (linux/arm, linux/386) ---
# Apple silicon has no AArch32 EL0 and the guest kernel is built without CONFIG_COMPAT, so a
# 32-bit ARM (or 32-bit x86) container image can ONLY run through an emulator. Rosetta covers
# linux/amd64; these cover the rest. vinit registers them in binfmt_misc at boot (setup_binfmt),
# so `docker run --platform linux/arm/v7 …` works out of the box with NO privileged
# `multiarch/qemu-user-static` / `tonistiigi/binfmt` setup container — and it survives restarts,
# which that container never can (its entries live in the guest kernel and die with the VM).
# Same shape as Docker Desktop: LinuxKit's pkg/binfmt bakes Debian's qemu user binaries into the
# VM image and registers them from init.
#
# SOURCE: Debian's `qemu-user` (NOT `qemu-user-static` — since Debian 13 that name is an empty
# transitional package of symlinks; the real, statically-linked binaries moved to `qemu-user`).
# Debian rather than Alpine deliberately: Alpine's user-mode QEMU is dynamically linked, which
# cannot work as a binfmt interpreter inside a container that has no matching /lib (LinuxKit
# carries the same note). They ship static-PIE and already stripped; the build asserts the two
# properties that would silently break emulation — no PT_INTERP, and an arm64 ELF.
# Which targets ship is chosen in guest/rootfs/Dockerfile (arm, i386, x86_64), NOT here.
# The .deb is fetched from deb.debian.org (fast CDN, but pool URLs vanish on the next point
# release) and falls back to the immutable snapshot.debian.org copy taken at QEMU_USER_SNAPSHOT;
# both are checked against the same SHA-256, so a mirror can never change what ships.
# PIN THE VERSION DEBIAN **STABLE** SERVES — not whatever is newest in the shared pool. The pool
# also holds testing/unstable uploads and binNMU rebuilds (`+b1`); adopting one of those ships a
# non-stable binary and its pool URL churns much faster. Scripts/check-upstream.sh watches this
# pin weekly and reads all three values from ONE small page (no 64 MB download in CI):
#   https://packages.debian.org/stable/arm64/qemu-user/download   (filename + SHA-256)
#   https://snapshot.debian.org/mr/binary/qemu-user/1%3A<ver>/binfiles?fileinfo=1   (stamp)
# QEMU_USER_BLOCKED is the deny list rollback.yml appends to, exactly like the other components.
QEMU_USER_VERSION=10.0.11+ds-0+deb13u1
QEMU_USER_SNAPSHOT=20260704T211317Z
QEMU_USER_DEB_SHA256=bb194fdc8c20a11b30e69cae469152942fb7921a0634dac5bea8e82cf6c807ae
QEMU_USER_BLOCKED=

# --- Docker CLI plugins (host-side Mac client, NOT guest components) ---
# `docker compose` and `docker buildx` are CLIENT-side CLI plugins: separate binaries
# the `docker` CLI discovers under its cli-plugins dirs. They run on the Mac and talk to
# the engine through the `velox` context — nothing here ships in the guest. Docker's mac
# static tarball contains ONLY `docker/docker` (no plugins), so Velox bundles these two
# itself: Scripts/build-app.sh downloads + SHA-verifies them into the app, and FirstRun
# links them into ~/.docker/cli-plugins (so a fresh Mac without Docker Desktop still has
# `docker compose` / `docker buildx`). Each is a bare arm64 binary from its OWN GitHub
# release — mind the DIFFERENT filenames/arch suffixes:
#   compose: https://github.com/docker/compose/releases/download/v<ver>/docker-compose-darwin-aarch64
#   buildx:  https://github.com/docker/buildx/releases/download/v<ver>/buildx-v<ver>.darwin-arm64
# No trusted checksum sidecar covers buildx's darwin binary, so pin the SHA-256 here
# (like DOCKER_CLI_MAC_ARM64_SHA256):  curl -fsSL <url> | shasum -a 256
DOCKER_COMPOSE_VERSION=5.5.0
DOCKER_COMPOSE_MAC_ARM64_SHA256=6777710e5a5db5709e5f4b5985844e74b73cc1a6123e3a6690b6ababf2deaf51
DOCKER_BUILDX_VERSION=0.36.1
DOCKER_BUILDX_MAC_ARM64_SHA256=214cdc36788602862dbc82b523d58648b4585c7b0ff95218b0817c44db5573d7

# --- Build-stage images + tools (NOT shipped in the guest) ---
# Velox uses NO Go (host is 100% Swift; guest is Rust-only). Build-stage toolchains:
# Rust (static vinit), Alpine (rootfs assembly + erofs), and Debian (the native arm64
# kernel-build toolchain — gcc/bc/bison/flex/libelf, in guest/kernel/Dockerfile.builder).
# Pinned by DIGEST, not tag. `rust:1-alpine` moves on every Rust patch release, and the
# guest/kernel cache keys hash these values as STRINGS — so a moved tag silently changed the
# shipped guest while the cache key stayed identical, and two builds of the same commit were
# not reproducible. The readable tag is kept in the comment; to bump, re-resolve with
# `docker buildx imagetools inspect <tag>` and replace the digest.
RUST_BUILD_IMAGE=rust@sha256:a10e64dd139b7387337c7fbe8aca31b959b57b2fd4c8ae20a02cf1d6ea424dce            # rust:1-alpine — builds the static vinit
ALPINE_IMAGE=alpine@sha256:fd791d74b68913cbb027c6546007b3f0d3bc45125f797758156952bc2d6daf40             # alpine:3.23 — rootfs assembly, static mkfs.ext4, CA bundle, mkfs.erofs
KERNEL_BUILDER_IMAGE=debian@sha256:3a39a0592364683e6bab97937b72cad5a8fa6dcbbee90edb3bb48c7f8e94f258      # debian:trixie-slim — kernel toolchain (Dockerfile.builder); Debian 13 = GCC 14
# moby/moby tag whose contrib/check-config.sh validates the kernel .config (build-kernel.sh).
# Pinned to a COMMIT, not the mutable v28.5.0 tag, and hashed: this script is fetched and
# executed as root inside the kernel-build container, which bind-mounts Assets/ read-write —
# so whoever controls its bytes controls the kernel that ships. Anyone able to move moby's
# tag had root RCE in the build before this pin. To bump: resolve the new tag to a commit,
# re-hash contrib/check-config.sh, update both lines.
MOBY_CHECKCONFIG_REF=cd048300a48700294339c9c91d2dcc691cb8f63b
MOBY_CHECKCONFIG_SHA256=6256f4de8f02798a8d35db16c7a41b2e582eef3ee521153451af112fd11b4e66

# Upstream versions the automation must NEVER adopt, space-separated. `rollback.yml`
# appends here when it rolls a bad release back; `Scripts/check-upstream.sh` skips anything
# listed. Without this, restoring a prior pin only lasted until the next weekly run, which
# re-adopted the exact version that had just been rolled back (CI's compile-only validation
# cannot see a boot/runtime failure — see CLAUDE.md §9's accepted limitation).
KERNEL_ORG_BLOCKED=
DOCKER_BLOCKED=
DOCKER_COMPOSE_BLOCKED=
DOCKER_BUILDX_BLOCKED=
